# Note: with a variable-length numeral code (an Elias-gamma length prefix per element) each
# element costs about 2*log2(b_k)+1 more bits, b_k ~ 4-27 bits needed (c2 table E): about 5-11
# bits per element, so ~25-35 bits per 4-element form and ~6-11 more for the 5-element vicarious
# hypothesis.  This shifts the vicarious-vs-4-element comparisons by ~6-11 bits and the 2-element
# circle against 4-element forms by ~12-20 bits; it does not change which forms are compatible.

## B. geocentric longitudes of Mars, 1580.0-1600.0 daily (Mars - EMB, J2000 ecliptic): what K3 is worth
# Earth start from a heliocentric ellipse fit: e=0.01688, period 365.256 d; Mars start from Part A; rho0 = 1.52369
  rho free (9 elements): minimax geocentric error 0.627'   rho = 1.523676, K3 value (n_E/n_M)^(2/3) = 1.523687
  K3       (8 elements): minimax geocentric error 0.632'
  (the planes are fixed: Earth in the J2000 ecliptic, Mars in the best heliocentric plane of Part A)
# K3 saving by the shear identity V8/V9 = p_Y(0), y = rho - (n_E/n_M)^(2/3), with hit-and-run on the
# exact compatible region (see k3_saving).  'K3 saves' = log2(range_rho * p_Y(0)), range_rho = 4, with
# p_Y(0) estimated as the fraction of samples with |y| < h over 2h, h = 0.05 / 0.1 / 0.2 sd(y); +-se over
# independent chains; in brackets the estimate from the first and the second half of the kept steps.
delta'    rho lo    rho hi     sd(y) y(minimax)  y q01..q99   K3 saves (bits), h = 0.05 / 0.1 / 0.2 sd
